// Two callers of `CodecFault`, written to falsify a prediction this directory made yesterday.
//
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` proposed that a fault union earns its members when more than one *kind* of
// caller exists, and then named two cases to check it against:
//
// > `CodecFault` (5) has one kind — a decoder either accepts base64 or does not — and should turn
// > out over-specified. `PageFault` (3) has two, a renderer and a test harness, and should not.
//
// **The first half is wrong, and it is wrong for the reason `@/packages/rlp` was.** Base64 has at
// least two kinds of caller and they were in front of me: a JWT reader, whose input is base64url and
// is *unpadded by specification* (RFC 7515 §2), and a MIME reader, where padding is required. Two
// specifications, one alphabet, opposite answers about one member.
//
// Written out, the five members split the same way RLP's seven did.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";
import {
  CodecFault, AtByte, AboutInput,
  NotADigit, ShortGroup, BadPadding, ImpossibleLength, NonZeroTail
} from "@/packages/codec";

/** Refuse, or decode anyway. */
export enum Strictness { Refuse, Allow }

/**
 * RFC 7515 §2: a JWT's segments are base64url **with the padding removed**.
 *
 * So the one member a MIME reader must refuse is the one this reader must accept, and it is not a
 * matter of taste — a JWT library that refuses unpadded input cannot read a JWT.
 */
export Strictness forJwt(CodecFault f) {
  match (f) {
    BadPadding(_):        { return Allow; }
    // Everything else stands, and `NonZeroTail` stands hardest: a signature is computed over the
    // text and verified against the bytes, so two texts decoding to one byte string is the whole
    // attack. `@/packages/codec`'s README argues this and a JWT is its sharpest case.
    NonZeroTail(_, _):    { return Refuse; }
    NotADigit(_, _):      { return Refuse; }
    ShortGroup(_):        { return Refuse; }
    ImpossibleLength(_):  { return Refuse; }
  }
}

/** RFC 2045: padding is required and a line that is not a multiple of four is corrupt. */
export Strictness forMime(CodecFault f) {
  match (f) {
    BadPadding(_):        { return Refuse; }
    NonZeroTail(_, _):    { return Refuse; }
    NotADigit(_, _):      { return Refuse; }
    ShortGroup(_):        { return Refuse; }
    ImpossibleLength(_):  { return Refuse; }
  }
}

/**
 * And the same policy after the split — **which is where the split turns out to be a choice.**
 *
 * `CodecFault` is `union<AtByte, AboutInput>` since 2026-09-05, so a caller that treats every
 * point-fault alike writes two arms rather than five. `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac` established
 * that a nested union is matchable as a group; this is the second file to use it and the first where
 * the grouping was designed rather than inherited.
 *
 * **And this file already had a different partition of the same five members, in §1.** Point-versus-
 * whole is not structural-versus-canonicality; they cut across each other:
 *
 *     |                    | AtByte | AboutInput |
 *     | structural         | NotADigit, ShortGroup   | ImpossibleLength |
 *     | canonicality       | BadPadding, NonZeroTail |    —             |
 *
 * A nested union can express **one** of them. Choosing `union<AtByte, AboutInput>` makes
 * `Err(is Canonicality):` unwritable, and choosing the other makes `f.at` unreachable — see §4.
 */
export Strictness forMimeGrouped(CodecFault f) {
  match (f) {
    Err(is AtByte):      { return Refuse; }
    Err(is AboutInput):  { return Refuse; }
  }
}

// ## 1. The prediction was wrong, and the shape that replaces it is the one RLP already had
//
// | | JWT | MIME |
// |---|---|---|
// | `BadPadding` | **Allow** | Refuse |
// | `NonZeroTail` | Refuse | Refuse |
// | `NotADigit`, `ShortGroup`, `ImpossibleLength` | Refuse | Refuse |
//
// One row of five differs, which is thin — and it is not thin in the way *over-specified* means. It
// is the same partition `@/packages/rlp` has:
//
//   * **Structural** — `NotADigit`, `ShortGroup`, `ImpossibleLength`. There are no bytes. Every
//     caller refuses, and a caller that wanted not to could not be given anything.
//   * **Canonicality** — `BadPadding`, `NonZeroTail`. **The bytes are there.** These say the text was
//     not the canonical encoding of them, and whether that matters is the caller's question.
//
// So the rule that survives two attempts is not about counting callers:
//
// > **A decode fault union earns its members at the structural/canonicality boundary and almost
// > nowhere else.** Structural members are interchangeable to every caller — they differ only in the
// > message. Canonical ones are where callers disagree, because a value exists and the question is
// > whether to hand it over.
//
// That is a *better* rule than the one it replaces, on three grounds: it is about the members rather
// than about how many consumers happen to exist in this tree, it predicted `RlpFault`'s split after
// the fact and `CodecFault`'s before writing this file, and it says which member to look at first in
// the twenty-eight unions that still have no caller.
//
// It also retires the caller-counting rule properly rather than quietly. `TimeFault`'s three callers
// really do differ six rows out of eight — but look at *which* six: `NoOffset`, `Trailing`,
// `NoSuchDate`, `OutOfRange`, `BadSeparator` are all cases where a date was recovered and the
// question is whether to hand it over, and `NotDigits` and `EmptyFraction`, the two constant rows,
// are the structural ones. **The same boundary, and I read it as caller-counting because there were
// three columns.**
//
// ## 2. What the two functions cost, which is the argument against doing this
//
// `forMime` is five arms answering `Refuse`. It exists so that adding a sixth member to `CodecFault`
// fails to compile here, and that is its whole value — it is a **completeness assertion spelled as a
// function**. `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac`'s `policy` had two dead arms and this has five constant
// ones, and both are the same thing: a `match` over a union is the only construct here that can be
// exhaustive, so anything wanting exhaustiveness has to be a `match`, even when it has nothing to
// decide.
//
// The alternative in most languages is a default arm, and a default arm is exactly what makes adding
// a member silent. So the cost is real and the fix is not *allow a default*.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **The two functions are one table and nothing says so.** Fifth file to write a per-caller policy as
// a `match` returning an enum, and the second where two policies over one union sit in one file with
// no way to state that they are columns of one thing. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has this from
// `@/packages/datetime/src/lenient.wac`, where there were three.
//
// **`Allow` needs the bytes and the `Err` does not have them.** `forJwt` answering `Allow` for
// `BadPadding` is a caller asking for a value that `Result<Bytes, CodecFault>` discarded when it
// took the `Err` branch. Second file in two units to hit this, and here it is unarguable rather than
// arguable: unpadded base64url is not an error being tolerated, it is **the specified input format**,
// so the decode that produced `BadPadding` should not have failed for this caller at all. The fault
// is being used to carry a policy question backwards, and the shape that removes it is a decoder
// parameterised by what it accepts — `decode(text, alphabet, Padding.Optional)` — which
// `@/packages/codec`'s `Padding` type already almost is.
//
// **Which makes `BadPadding` the first member in this directory that a caller should not be able to
// receive.** If the decoder takes the policy, a JWT reader configures `Padding.Optional` and
// `BadPadding` is unreachable for it — not allowed, *impossible*. That is the same *dead arm* the
// `Mount` entry could not remove, arriving from the other side: here the caller does know, at
// compile time, which decoder it configured, so a `decode` whose fault type depended on its padding
// argument would remove the arm. That is a return type computed from an argument value, which is not
// in `../../../GRAMMAR.md` and is the third distinct place this directory has wanted one.

// ## 4. Nesting picks one partition and this union has two
//
// The split above was made for a *field* reason: `../../../QUESTIONS.md` counted 14 unions here where
// a position is carried by most members and not all, and grouping the point-faults is what would let
// a caller read `at` without matching. §1's split was made for a *caller* reason: `forJwt` and
// `forMime` differ on exactly the canonicality members and agree on everything else.
//
// Both are real and they are orthogonal, and `union<A, B>` nests in one direction only:
//
// > **A union with two useful partitions can spell one of them.** The members are a set; the grouping
// > is a tree; and there is no way to say *these four share a field* and *these two are where callers
// > disagree* about the same five values.
//
// Which is the same shape as `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *`Mount`'s funcref struct forbids
// per-implementation error sets* — a decision that buys one thing forecloses another, and the
// foreclosure is invisible until somebody wants the second. Here it took one file wanting both, and
// the file is this one.
//
// **The honest reading is that the split above may be the wrong one.** The caller reason has two
// callers behind it and the field reason has none — nothing here reads `at` off a `CodecFault` at
// all. A grouping chosen for a use nobody has, over one two functions in this file demonstrate, is
// the ordering this directory has argued against everywhere else.
